\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The hierarchical family}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Fix an integer #{p\ge 2} and real numbers #{\lambda>0} and
#{m>0}. \ref{fdip-00C3} gives a probability law on coherent
residue sequences whose coordinate at each depth is the
fixed-ratio Gaussian, together with the exact mean relation
that defines those sequences. Coefficients that are
eventually geometric in the sense of \ref{fdip-00C6} have
rescaled block means converging to that fixed-ratio law.}

\p{At a fixed depth, the expected squared increment depends
only on the congruence depth of the two sites
(\ref{fdip-00C5}). When #{\lambda=1} that increment equals
#{2} for every pair of distinct sites. When #{\lambda<1} it
is strictly decreasing in the congruence depth. When
#{\lambda>1} it is strictly increasing on distinct sites,
and equal sites contribute #{0}. Each depth produces a
different value when #{\lambda} differs from #{1}.}

\p{For #{0<p\lambda<1}, \ref{fdip-00FG} gives a square-integrable
cell density, and \ref{fdip-00FI} makes its increment an
ultrametric. The covering sums of that ultrametric are computed
in \ref{fdip-00FK}. For #{p\lambda\ge 1}, \ref{fdip-00FL}
excludes a square-integrable point density whose cylinder
averages match every depth at once. The projective law of
\ref{fdip-00C3} remains for every #{\lambda>0}.}
